Z||000028||b lo 0||07-21-2000||01:41 PM||||For ROCKCRAWL's information, a 512TR is a Testarossa-it's the same car.  The 512TR is kinda like the model designation, and Testarossa was the project name which stuck.  TR in 512TR actually stands for Testarossa.  trust me on this, I love Ferraris and I am considering a 355 kit for my '85 GT.  Oh, good analogy, it's the same way a 355 is called the 355 and the Maranello.  Have fun, yall!<P>------------------<BR>'85 GT (in progress)  <BR>titled ICE-plates B LO 0 <BR>GAWD I love this car||||reg||1
Z||000029||loafer87gt||07-21-2000||04:09 PM||||Err, hate to be a nitpicker but the Maranello is the Ferrari 550, the larger GT coupe.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Anyways, I love the 512 Fascia, but whats with the little scoop on the lower right side? I'm assuming its for air cooling. I wonder if PISA would ever import these for sale?||||reg||1
Z||000030||b lo 0||07-21-2000||04:32 PM||||You know what I meant, loafer!!||||reg||1
Z||000031||dman||07-21-2000||06:25 PM||||Yeah, I know I'm nitpicking. But b lo 0, I'm pretty sure rockcrawl knows that a 512TR is a Testarossa, but a Testarossa may not necessarily be a 512TR. Both rockcrawl and myself are each building Testarossa replicas so you can be assured that I've done my homework on these cars.<P>There were 3 Testarossa autombiles. The original Testarossa that came out in '84 (it is known as a Testarossa), the 512TR of '91 which had a new front and rear, and then the F512M (introduced in '95 I think, M for modified, headlights no longer popped up, new front and rear, in fact the front looks very similar to that used on the 355, and more changes).<P>All of them were pretty cool. <BR>||||reg||1
